# C64/C128: stage the same vector weight layout as a separate experiment

This is a CPU-reviewed, uncompiled draft. It has no measured speedup, native
parity result or public selection policy. The C256 cache, B-prefetch and QKV
experiments remain separate. All new files end in `.draft`.

## What the existing profiles show

The old C64 profile uses real block 5, a 288×480 field, phase 1 and the
unrolled baseline. The C128 profile uses real block 9, a 272×480 field, phase 1
and variant 3 (rolled K with coalesced packed stores). Both have packed input
and output and omit the raw side output. They use different schedules and
fields, so this table is context, not a cross-width speed comparison.

| Existing NCU observation | C64 baseline | C128 variant 3 |
|---|---:|---:|
| Profiled duration | 57.184 µs | 134.848 µs |
| Registers/thread | 186 | 164 |
| Allocated shared memory | 5,120 B | 9,216 B |
| Active warp percentage | 16.50% | 24.94% |
| Tensor elapsed utilization | 29.37% | 41.87% |
| L2 throughput | 12.17% | 28.99% |
| DRAM throughput | 9.33% | 7.38% |
| Global-load L1 hit rate | 86.52% | 81.41% |
| L2 read hit rate | 78.78% | 89.87% |

The full/source replays have separate sample totals. The C64 **source** replay
has 1,269 long-scoreboard samples; C128 has 5,106. Their full-replay totals are
1,522 and 4,845 respectively. Do not merge those populations.

For C128, the largest source hotspot (463 samples) is a QMMA at
`0x1201541840` consuming `R66/R67`; eight scalar `LDG.E` instructions at
`0x1201541740..0x12015417b0` load its four B pairs. Another 317-sample hotspot
maps to `dense32_step`. These support reducing B-load instruction and address
work. C64 also has an MMA/weight-load hotspot (78 samples), but its largest
hotspot is an initial-input dependency at `STS.128` (317 samples). This
experiment does not alter that input staging.

The existing reports and exact SASS neighborhoods are pinned in
[`window_small_vector_weight_proof.json.draft`](../outputs/window_small_vector_weight_proof.json.draft).
Neither profile approaches the requested 85% roofline gate. High L1 hit rates
also caution against interpreting fewer sector requests as an equal drop in
DRAM traffic or latency.

## Mapping and implementation

The existing cache already supports the smaller matrix shapes. It stores a
canonical snapshot in slice 0 and a vector layout in slice 1:

```text
[group][K/32][N/16][lane32][four uint32 words]
```

There is no K permutation. Shared A fragments retain canonical channel order.
For lane `l`, N32 row group `r` and K32 group `kk`, the two vector addresses
inside a matrix are:

```text
kk*N*32 + (r/16 + tile)*512 + l*16,   tile = 0,1
```

Each vector is aligned to 16 bytes. Its four words are exactly the original
two K16 halves for two adjacent N8 fragments. All W1 experts and W2 experts
are packed independently; W3, QKV and projection are single matrices.

| Per-block source/address model | C64 | C128 |
|---|---:|---:|
| Warps per CTA | 2 | 4 |
| Shared A bytes | 4,096 | 8,192 |
| Dense K32 iterations | 2 | 4 |
| Matrix bytes in one cache slice | 45,056 | 163,840 |
| Dual-cache allocation bytes | 90,112 | 327,680 |
| Scalar B-load instructions/warp before | 176 | 320 |
| Vector B-load instructions/warp after | 44 | 80 |

For one K32/N32 B group, the source model replaces eight scalar loads with
two 128-bit loads. The sum of separately requested 32-byte sectors drops from
64 to 32. Repeated cached requests count in that model; it is not measured
L2/DRAM traffic. Source shared storage excludes the compiler/driver allocation
overhead visible in NCU.

The kernel and launcher are copies of the isolated C256 vector-cache baseline
with separate names and a C64/C128 contract. After reversing those changes,
all source text is identical. Cache preparation is also identical under a
separate private API, avoiding any change to the existing cache entry point.
The four variants preserve the baseline schedule choices: unrolled, rolled K,
coalesced packed stores, and both. No prefetch, QKV fusion, arithmetic change,
extra shared storage or barrier is added. All seven barriers stay in place.

The private APIs are `_prepare_window_small_vector_weight` and
`_inference_window_block_small_vector_weight`. They retain guarded current
stream launches, device and full-shape checks, no-autograd checks, alignment
repair, Half/packed activation storage and optional raw output. Weights remain
FP8; this experiment does not change FP16-weight inference. Variants 2/3
require packed output, while variants 0/1 also support Half publication.

## What is proved, and what is pending

[`window_small_vector_weight_proof.py.draft`](../tools/window_small_vector_weight_proof.py.draft)
checks all 208,896 cache bytes across the two widths, every aligned vector
boundary, group boundaries and the original scalar-word coordinates. It checks
104,448 lane-MMA events with unchanged ascending K order. Cache generation is
a byte permutation, and source normalization confirms unchanged arithmetic,
publication, shared storage and barriers. This CPU proof passed.

The test draft collects **284 cases** without running GPU work. Coverage
includes both cache slices, all weight families, all phases and variants,
Half/packed input and output modes, raw/no-raw outputs, batch 2, spatial tails,
encoder and decoder checkpoint weights, offset/noncontiguous data, mutated
state/scales/QKV cache during graph replay, poisoned retained outputs, poisoned
cache-preparation replay, empty batches and invalid/autograd contracts.

The benchmark compares all compatible old canonical variants, the earlier
pair-prefetch route, public selection, both resident composed paths and the
four small-cache variants. Each receives the same logical inputs and retained
outputs, graph call count and timing method. Cache preparation is outside
resident timing and its bytes are disclosed. Candidate order uses a recorded
seed; `--reverse` repeats it in reverse. Defaults derive field dimensions from
720p, 1080p and 4K geometry for representative encoder/decoder blocks. Half
publication selects only compatible variants. Speed is compared with the
fastest existing route, and the draft reports a >3% improvement gate.

Compilation must first establish that the intended 128-bit B loads survive,
that extra address registers do not lower occupancy, and that no spills are
introduced. C64 already uses 186 registers/thread in the sampled baseline;
its smaller CTA is not evidence of low register pressure. Rolled K can bound
live ranges but adds loop work, and its trip counts are only two/four at these
widths. The compiler may hoist vector values differently from scalar loads.

Only after exact local correctness, sanitizer and graph gates pass should the
resident timing run. A winner still needs native physical-layout parity/timing
and a fresh source/full NCU profile before any public-policy proposal. This
draft neither promotes a route nor claims the 85% hardware gate is met.

Files to activate only after review:

- `csrc/kernel_impl/window_block_small_vector_weight.cuh.draft`
- `csrc/kernel_launcher/window_block_small_vector_weight.cu.draft`
- `csrc/torch_api/window_block_small_vector_weight.cpp.draft`
- `tests/test_window_small_vector_weight.py.draft`
- `tests/benchmark_window_small_vector_weight.py.draft`

Generation and proof entry points are
`tools/draft_window_small_vector_weight.py.draft` and
`tools/window_small_vector_weight_proof.py.draft`. Both are CPU-only.
